.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_DYNAMIC_ALLOCS_COUNT 3 "2026-09-15" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count \- report outstanding fallback allocations
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

size_t ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count(ccol_mempool *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the number of outstanding allocations that came from the system
allocator instead of the storage of the pool. These happen only in a
pool created with fallback_to_dynamic_memory = true. They happen only
after the elements of the pool run out.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp                        Memory pool to query (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the number of outstanding fallback allocations. It returns 0 for
a pool created without a fallback
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A non-zero result means that the pool was too small for its workload at
some point. It is the signal to raise elem_count. Every fallback
allocation is one round trip to the system allocator, and that is the
cost which the pool exists to avoid.

An entry in the cache of a thread is pool-owned. This call never counts
such an entry.

ccol_mempool_destroy() treats an outstanding fallback allocation as a
leak and stops the process. Free every fallback entry of a pool with a
fallback before you destroy that pool.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
The call takes the lock of the pool. It is therefore a cold-path call.
Do not use it inside a hot loop.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_total_capacity (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_used_count (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3)
